# MOONSHOTS — Next-Generation Architectural Horizons & Advanced Capabilities
Floor: 2
Thrust: 5
Patch: 6
File: openOODA/MOONSHOTS.oot

This document defines long-term architectural horizons and advanced research milestones.
All future tracks align with the six strategic pillars and the five evolution phases.
Active sprint tasks, the release pin (v2.5.6), and near-term deliverables reside in ROADMAP.oot.

## 0. Strategic Architecture Alignment & Evolution Phases

The openOODA technical horizon advances through five evolutionary phases under Generational SemVer:
- Phase 1 (Kinematic to Informational OODA): Elevates instruction optimization to AST observation and SMT contract orientation.
- Phase 2 (Centralized to Distributed State Mesh): Implements sensor fusion consensus and peer-to-peer capability verification.
- Phase 3 (Static Code to Autonomous Adaptation): Implements real-time AST mutation, live PGO, and transactional memory execution.
- Phase 4 (Boundary Physics Limits): Enforces mathematical bounds through Rice's theorem, CAP theorem, and Landauer's principle.
- Phase 5 (Generational Architecture Encapsulation): Stabilizes Gen 1-4 libraries under Gen 5 teams and Gen 6 autonomous synthesis.
- Power Law 80/20 Floor Rule: Major architectural transitions advance the Generational Floor (F=3 to F=6).

## 1. Phase 1: Informational OODA & Subtractive Optimization

- Subtractive Drag Elimination Engine (Pillar 2 E-M Engine):
  Systematically eliminate computational drag (D -> 0) across compiler and runtime pipelines.
  Replace dynamic heap allocations with linear arenas and O(1) bulk resets.
  Elide runtime contract checks through compile-time SMT proof generation.

- Full-Spectrum AST Observation & Merkle Hashing (Pillar 2 Informational E-M):
  Ingest source code changes as cryptographic Merkle AST digests.
  Compress orientation latency from O(N) full scans to O(log N) subtree diffs.
  Enable sub-millisecond OODA loop turnover across agent teams.

- Automated Binary Hardening & Telemetry Stripping (Pillar 5 Capability Security):
  Ingest foreign legacy binaries (C/C++, Rust, Go, Zig, WASM).
  Statically trace call graphs and concolic syscall reachability.
  Strip ambient tracking, telemetry, and ungranted system access.
  Re-emit hardened native OODA binaries with explicit capability tokens.

## 2. Phase 2: Distributed State Mesh & Consensus Protocols

- Byzantine Fault Tolerant Capability DAG Consensus (Pillar 6 Swarm Coordination):
  Coordinate heterogeneous agent teams over a decentralized capability DAG.
  Converge distributed sensor fusion and state transitions in O(log M) communication rounds.
  Authenticate inter-agent wire framing using Noise XX handshakes and ChaCha20-Poly1305 seals.

- Peer-to-Peer SMT Proof Verification Mesh (Pillar 3 Negative Trust):
  Distribute contract verification workloads across peer agent nodes.
  Verify SMT proofs and capability grants independently before committing state.
  Prevent forged capability propagation across untrusted network nodes.

- Decentralized Zero-Knowledge Verifiable Compute (Pillar 5 Capability Security):
  Pair SECRET field taint tracking with cryptographic ZK-STARK execution proofs.
  Execute code on untrusted decentralized cloud nodes with mathematical proof of contract compliance.
  Protect private data without exposing secret fields to untrusted hosts.

## 3. Phase 3: Autonomous Adaptation & Real-Time Mutation

- Real-Time AST Mutation & Live Machine Code Emission (Pillar 6 Autonomous Adaptation):
  Autonomous worker fibers detect runtime execution bottlenecks.
  Mutate AST subtrees in memory (e.g. convert Array-of-Structs to Struct-of-Arrays for SIMD).
  Prove safety against SMT preconditions and re-emit native binary code without downtime.

- Live Profile-Guided Optimization Feedback Loop (Pillar 2 E-M Thrust):
  Feed runtime branch bias, loop trip counts, and cache telemetry back into the SSA optimizer.
  Reorder basic blocks and propagate dynamic constants in running binary images.

- Transactional Rollback Memory (Pillar 3 Adversarial Hardening):
  Execute speculative mutations inside isolated micro-transactional memory buffers.
  On contract violation or memory error, roll back state changes instantly with an O(1) pointer reset.
  Attenuate offending capabilities and commit only verified safe state transitions.

- Sub-Millisecond Autonomous One-Turn Repair (Pillar 6 Multi-Agent Coordination):
  Eliminate human cognitive delay bottlenecks from the feedback loop.
  Detect compile or runtime errors, synthesize AST patches, and certify fixes in one turn.

## 4. Phase 4: Boundary Physics & Mathematical Invariants

- Rice-Bounded Decidable Contract Synthesis (Pillar 1 First Principles):
  Enforce Rice's theorem boundaries by restricting contracts to decidable First-Order Logic fragments (QF_LIA, QF_BV).
  Apply deterministic SMT solver timeouts and fail closed on unverified contracts.

- Asynchronous Monotonic CAP Leases (Pillar 5 Zero Ambient Authority):
  Prioritize Consistency and Safety over Availability under network partitions.
  Enforce monotonic local capability attenuation (ChildCap subset of ParentCap).
  Expire capability leases automatically into default-deny states during communication loss.

- Landauer Minimal Thermodynamic Entropy Teardowns (Pillar 1 Silicon Invariants):
  Minimize thermodynamic bit erasure entropy (Delta Q >= kB * T * ln 2).
  Structure memory in contiguous linear arenas with O(1) bulk pointer resets.
  Implement zero-leak deterministic RAII ARC teardowns without garbage collection pauses.

## 5. Phase 5: Architecture Encapsulation & Native Microkernel

- Autonomic Reflex Layer Encapsulation (Pillar 1 First Principles):
  Encapsulate Gen 1 (Silicon HAL), Gen 2 (Compiler IR), Gen 3 (Runtime Memory), and Gen 4 (Type System) into stable libraries.
  Allow Gen 5 (Multi-Agent Teams) and Gen 6 (Autonomous Synthesis) to operate on high-level goals.

- Bare-Metal Native Microkernel (Pillar 1 Hardware Foundations):
  Implement a bare-metal operating system kernel entirely in pure `.oo`.
  Directly map hardware interrupts, MMU page tables (PML4/Sv39), and device controllers to capability tokens.
  Execute agent swarms directly on hardware without host operating system layers.

- Autonomous Intent Synthesis & Binary Hardening (Pillar 6 Intent Synthesis):
  Synthesize high-level specifications and formal contracts into verified native machine code.
  Continuously mutate binary layouts, CFI shadow stacks, and call-edge hashes to prevent exploitation.
